e the legacy  code the stuff was scared to change the  reason twe scared to change it is that  it actually works pretty well  it's in production it's it's purring  away smoothly no one can remember how it  got there or what it's doing while it's  there but it's live and the money is  coming in and people are buying things  and you know making movies or selling  airline tickets or whatever it is that  your organization is doing and so we  have this kind of weird situations of  stuff where you very rapidly get  familiarity is often the stuff which is  not terribly good in the first place the  stuff which surprises you is when  someone's like the system's down you're  like I didn't even know we had that  system and they're like yeah made us a  couple of million quid last year you're  like really I didn't know about it yet  it never broke just kind of put away  quietly like the brake light here on  your BMW and so one of the most valuable  techniques we can use to promote  understanding of these kinds of systems  is transparency we want to go into the  the systems which are working the ones  that everyone has kind of forgotten that  they're even there and we want to say  well what are they doing you know I  always loved the fact in Star Trek The  Next Generation the engine is part of  the set there is this huge thing in the  middle now I I happen to know because  it's inside a show business knowledge  that the engine here is an ultraviolet  disco light in a tank full of gin and  tonic because that's what gives it that  was how they got that blue that  Cherenkov radiation effect on on TV  shows and stuff but you know this was  part of the set and if you ever watched  Star Trek The Next Generation and you  saw the engine room and this thing was  not glowing blue you knew exactly what  was going on and that that was the plot  of this episode is the enterprise is  crippled the engines are down what's  happened why isn't it glowing what  happened to the gin and tonic you know  there's a problem here we want to try  and create the same you know level of  transparency and awareness you have  these systems that never go wrong  put sitting on the equivalent of you  know dashboard lights on your car is the  stock control system working of course  it's working it's been working for 10  years yes but is it work  today is the first indicator we have  that there's a problem and the capacity  of it and we wait to get an angry phone  call are we about to run out of database  space you know we have legacy systems  where someone put them in place 10 years  ago they work perfectly  one day someone decommissioned the email  that would tell you if it was running  out of disk space and it's just sitting  there quietly logging a couple of  transactions a day to a C Drive which  has I don't know 64 gigs of capacity as  that was a lot back in the days when we  provision these kinds of systems and you  know I think a big part of this we as an  industry have to accept some collective  responsibility for the challenges that  we face because during certainly during  the 1980s and 1990s the kind of first  wave of business software and business  software automation we as an industry  went around telling people buy computers  get a paperless office integrate  everything you can fire all of your  staff and you can run your entire  company from this one room we have one  button and you we push it for you before  your home you know we sold this prospect  of business automation being this  magical you know we automate all of the  processes and then it just it runs by  magic and we as much as anybody are  responsible for this idea of software  projects like you know a project which  has a start and a finish and then after  it's finished the problem is solved and  it's fine you know  it's we've built something and once the  thing is built it just runs forever and  this is why we encounter this friction  in organizations where they're like why  do we have 20 software engineers on  payroll we are not a software company we  sell tires and you're like you don't  sell tires the software sells tires you  know your job is finding people you can  plug into the software and the 20  engineers are there because every time  you find a person then know exactly like  the people who were involved in the case  study when you wrote the thing in the  first place you know you look at any  kind of classic textbook on on business  automation and something that will we'll  come across a lot is manual processes  have flexibility in them  you know you're going to say check into  a hotel you're like I'm allergic to  carrots they're like no problem they  make a note on the menu they send that  through to the kitchen the chef looks at  it like okay no carrots for you that's  fine you go to a Hertz on that as an  iPad touchscreen you like room this  phone must occurred where's the allergic  to carrots but  it doesn't exist so you're like I have  an allergy to carrots and they're like  oh hang on let me see there's no button  I looked you know and that kind of  flexibility when you automate a process  what you do is you automate one person  or at one team's snapshot of how that  process worked on one particular day  when you captured that set of  requirements and this is changing people  are getting used to the idea you know  agile lean and Kanban have played a big  part in this and like it you don't tell  us what you want and then we ship it you  tell us what you want now we'll give it  to you tomorrow you tell us what you  want the day after that and we'll keep  going around that process for as long as  it takes or we'll come back as often as  is necessary so that you can keep  evolving you can keep developing the  things that you're doing it's not easy  keeping track of all the stuff you know  because we have this perception of you  know software breaks we got to monitor  it we've got to do this we got to invest  in maintaining it we've got to invest in  keeping the whole thing running and a  lot of the times you look at  organizations and you think your  accounting model here maybe isn't quite  right you've got 20 software engineers  on staff who are maintaining a system  that you cannot actually track the value  streams through this organization and  work out whether you're selling enough  tires I'm using tires as a hypothetical  example by the way that you don't know  whether you're selling enough tires to  cover that you just know that the  software is important because that's  what you were told you have to sell  tires because that's what you do and if  you don't have the engineers the  software will stop working and you will  have legacy code incidents but often the  most valuable thing that you can do is  you can look at a system you go this  doesn't make sense anymore  this should no longer be here let's  start trimming away some of the fat some  of the unnecessary you'll upset some  customers you know I had a situation  once where we wanted to upgrade a  front-end on a page a feature on one of  our websites and we're kind of digging  through the logs and it's like we got  five people who are still using build of  Firefox that wouldn't work on it and  we're like ok how long's it gonna take  to make all this stuff work in Firefox  build 38 or whatever it was it was like  a couple of weeks I was like what's it  gonna cost to get a couple of bottles of  really good single malt scotch and we'll  send someone to each of those people be  like hello we're from spotlight we  brought your bottle of whiskey and we're  gonna upgrade  your computer while you drink it you  know just think of other ways of solving  the problem ways of eliminating moving  the complexity out of the codebase maybe  you just decide we're not doing that  anymore the eight people who use this  feature are gonna be upset and they  might you know say nasty things about us  on Twitter but we can do that where  grown-ups here we can handle a little  bit of controversy because this is the  right decision for what we are doing  think about a definition of done the  code is deleted the github repositories  are archived the database is offline the  servers are decommissioned the teams at  the beach drinking beers this is the  software system that is not going to  wake you up at 4 o'clock on Sunday  morning this is what a finished software  project actually looks like we must have  the confidence the trust the  understanding and the control that are  necessary to go into the code and say  this is the right thing to do you know  and the world that we live in today  it runs on software software it's  absolutely everywhere we cannot get away  from it we cannot rewrite it we don't  know what it does we can't switch it off  we're relying on it so like I said you  know book our train tickets and sell us  tires and washer dryers and all of the  things that we rely on day after day  after day and the vast majority of the  code that runs the world is legacy code  it is VB and it's Excel spreadsheets and  it's dotnet web forms that was written  before people really knew how to do unit  testing properly we need to get better  at how we'd cope with it writing code is  fun you know downhill invention it's  brilliant you sit there with your little  feedback loop on your mental model and  it's an incredibly rewarding exercise  and that's brilliant for prototyping for  R&amp;D for experiments for validating new  concepts and all kinds of things but all  of the time the way that we actually  create the most value the way that we  change the world we live in is not  inventing new stuff it is by helping us  and the people around us get better at  working with all the stuff that already  exists and may be the solution to legacy  code has been staring us in the face  this whole time because what we need to  do is control it bring it into a sandbox  get confident alter it get to a point  where we know how to make changes and  eventually when the time is right and  the understanding is there delete it  thank you very much  we have a few minutes for questions I  believe which might mean somebody comes  running around with a microphone or  might not I don't know okay are there  any questions or have we achieved total  enlightenment cool well thank you all  very much for coming  I'm around all day come and chat if you  want to talk about legacy code or  made-up programming languages or any of  that kind of stuff I don't kind of pub  conf  yeah but I see you today I see a pop gun  if not I'll see you next time thanks a  lot have a good day
 

